  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   We aim to improve upon the current upper bound of C\u22480.380861 by employing a more sophisticated optimization procedure that combines polynomial methods with scalable parameter optimization. The core idea is to parameterize the step function using coefficients of orthogonal polynomials evaluated at fixed points, enabling the use of gradient-based optimization with a reduced search space. We address limitations in the original approach through four key enhancements:\n   - Polynomial representation to capture functional shape variations\n   - Automatic integral constraint handling via orthogonal bases\n   - Bounded-value encoding using sigmoid transformations\n   - Progressive optimization from global to local phases\n   We believe this approach can find better configurations than local optimization alone.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Discretize [0,2] into n points, with h stored as coefficients in a vector\u03b8 \u2208 \u211d^m\n   - Map coefficients to function values using \u03b8 \u21a6 sigmoid(a_i \u03b8_i + b_i) for each grid point\n   - Orthogonal polynomial basis P2,P3,P5,P7 (optimizing degree adaptive) satisfies \u222bPmPn dx = \u03b4mn\n   - Objective: C5 = (1/(n dx)) * \u27e8correlate(proj(\u03c3(\u03b8)), \u03c3(\u03b8))\u27e9_\u221e\n   - Constraints: \u222bh(x)dx=1 automatically via orthogonal norm \u2227\n   - Support: Bounded outputs via sigmoid, avoiding clipping\n   - Normalization: Use orthogonal polynomial coefficients for unit energy basis\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   Phase 1: Batched initialization\n     - Start with either provided parent or uniform grid\n     - For parent: Map \u03b8_uniform through orthogonal basis\n     - For new: Use random normal initialization\n     - Apply independentflip normalization to enforce integral=1\n\n   Phase 2: Multi-stage optimization\n     - Stage 1: Bayesian optimization (GPyOpt) with constraints handled via penalization\n     - Stage 2: Gradient descent (L-BFGS-B) using autograd-proximal projection\n     - Stage 3: Multi-start local search at tentacle points\n\n   Phase 3: Progressive refinement\n     - Start with coarse grid (n=100), then subdivide to n=200\n     - Repeat until deadline or n=1000 reached\n     - Parthenogenesis via interpolation-based continuation\n\n   Phase 4: Symmetry handling\n     - Compute symmetric candidate if primary improvement stalls\n     - Combine with parent via weighted geometric mean\n\n   Phase 5: Dimensionality management\n     - For grids <600: Scale up interpolation\n     - For grids above parent's n: Downsample parent if similar score found\n\n   Phase 6: Timeout fallback\n     - Prioritize retention of nearest neighbor solution\n     - Use Last-Value-Wins strategy\n\n4. Search choices:\n   Key Configurations:\n   - Polynomial bases: Use Gram polynomials truncated at \u03c0=\u221e\n   - Sigmoid parameters: a_i \u2208 [-1,1], b_i = -0.5*\u03c0/2\n   - Optimization stages: Bayesian phase (300s), gradient phase (600s), local phase (300s)\n   - Grid progression: Staged by powers of 1.3 (e.g., 200, 338, etc.)\n   - Regret threshold: 0.004 improvement window from parent\n\n5. Compute budget:\n   Full cycle: ~1800s for the most rigorous 200\u2192338\u2192502\u2192686 progression\n   Safeguards: Add 300s overhead per dimension increase\n   Monitoring: Check performance decay and switch to fallback if 2:1 speedup observed\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Post-process: Compute final C5 using numpy correlation\n   - Ensure integral=nearest(n_points/2) via counterpropagation\n   - Maintain output format as (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n   - Handle dimension changes through interpolation schemes\n   - Fallback criteria: 3 consecutive failed convergence attempts",
